.TH bhist 1 "Volclava Version 2.2.1 - Oct 2026"
.br
.SH NAME
\fBbhist\fR - displays historical information about jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbhist\fR [\fB-a\fR] [\fB-d\fR] [\fB-e\fR] [\fB-p\fR] [\fB-s\fR] [\fB-r\fR]
[\fB-b\fR | \fB-l\fR] [\fB-w\fR]
[\fB-f\fR \fIlogfile_name\fR | \fB-n\fR \fInum_logfiles\fR |
\fB-n\fR \fImin_logfile\fR\fB,\fR\fImax_logfile\fR]
[\fB-C\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR] [\fB-S\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR]
[\fB-D\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR]
[\fB-N\fR \fIhost_name\fR | \fB-N\fR \fIhost_model\fR | \fB-N\fR \fICPU_factor\fR]
[\fB-P\fR \fIproject_name\fR] [\fB-q\fR \fIqueue_name\fR] [\fB-m\fR \fIhost_name\fR]
[\fB-J\fR \fIjob_name\fR] [\fB-u\fR \fIuser_name\fR | \fB-u all\fR]
[\fIjob_ID\fR | \fB"\fR\fIjob_ID\fR\fB[\fR\fIindex\fR\fB]"\fR ...]
.PP
\fBbhist\fR \fB-t\fR [\fB-f\fR \fIlogfile_name\fR] [\fB-T\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR]
.PP
\fBbhist\fR [\fB-h\fR | \fB-V\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Displays the history of jobs, read from the event log file lsb.events.
To display the current state of jobs, use bjobs.
.PP
By default, bhist:
.IP \(bu 2
displays only your own jobs;
.IP \(bu 2
displays only unfinished jobs, that is, pending, running and suspended jobs;
.IP \(bu 2
reads only the current event log file, LSB_SHAREDIR/logdir/lsb.events,
and not the archived files lsb.events.1, lsb.events.2 and so on;
.IP \(bu 2
displays how long each job spent in each state (see OUTPUT).
.PP
When job IDs are specified, bhist displays those jobs whatever their owner
and state, and ignores -u, -q, -m, -S, -D, -C and the state options -a, -d,
-e, -p, -s and -r.
.PP
mbatchd periodically archives lsb.events, so the events of older jobs are
only in the archived files. Use -n to search them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB-a\fR
Displays jobs in all states, including finished jobs.
.TP
\fB-d\fR
Displays only finished jobs, both those that finished successfully (DONE)
and those that exited (EXIT).
.TP
\fB-e\fR
Displays only jobs that exited (EXIT).
.TP
\fB-p\fR
Displays only pending jobs.
.TP
\fB-s\fR
Displays only suspended jobs, suspended by the user or by the system.
.TP
\fB-r\fR
Displays only running jobs.
.PP
.RS
The state options can be combined; for example, -ps displays pending and
suspended jobs. -a overrides the other state options.
.RE
.TP
\fB-b\fR
Brief format. Lists the events of each job in time order, showing only the
main information of each event.
.TP
\fB-l\fR
Long format. Lists the events of each job in time order in detail, followed
by a summary of the time the job spent in each state. -l overrides -b.
.TP
\fB-w\fR
Wide format. Fields are not truncated.
.TP
\fB-f\fR \fIlogfile_name\fR
Reads only the specified event log file. Specify an absolute or a relative
path. Useful for offline analysis. Cannot be used with -n.
.TP
\fB-n\fR \fInum_logfiles\fR
Reads \fInum_logfiles\fR event log files, starting with the current
lsb.events and going back through the most recent archived files. For
example, -n 1 reads only lsb.events, and -n 3 reads lsb.events, lsb.events.1
and lsb.events.2. -n 0 reads all event log files, up to 100.
.IP
The archived files must be numbered consecutively; if a file is missing,
the files older than it are not read.
.TP
\fB-n\fR \fImin_logfile\fR\fB,\fR\fImax_logfile\fR
Reads the archived event log files numbered from \fImin_logfile\fR to
\fImax_logfile\fR. For example, -n 2,5 reads lsb.events.2 through
lsb.events.5.
.TP
\fB-C\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR
Displays only jobs that finished during the specified time interval. Implies
-d; with -e, displays only jobs that exited.
.IP
Specify the interval as:
.IP
\fIyyyy\fR/\fImm\fR/\fIdd\fR/\fIHH\fR:\fIMM\fR\fB,\fR\fIyyyy\fR/\fImm\fR/\fIdd\fR/\fIHH\fR:\fIMM\fR
.IP
\fIyyyy\fR is the four-digit year, \fImm\fR the month (1 to 12), \fIdd\fR the
day (1 to 31), \fIHH\fR the hour (0 to 23), and \fIMM\fR the minute (0 to
59). Do not use spaces in the interval. For example,
2026/10/29/00:00,2026/10/30/23:59 is from Oct 29 2026 00:00 to Oct 30 2026
23:59.
.TP
\fB-S\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR
Displays only jobs that were submitted during the specified time interval.
The interval format is the same as for -C.
.TP
\fB-D\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR
Displays only jobs that were dispatched during the specified time interval.
The interval format is the same as for -C.
.TP
\fB-N\fR \fIhost_name\fR | \fB-N\fR \fIhost_model\fR | \fB-N\fR \fICPU_factor\fR
Normalizes CPU time by the specified CPU factor, or by the CPU factor of the
specified host or host model. Use lsinfo to see host models and CPU factors.
.TP
\fB-P\fR \fIproject_name\fR
Displays only jobs that belong to the specified project.
.TP
\fB-q\fR \fIqueue_name\fR
Displays only jobs that were submitted to the specified queue.
.TP
\fB-m\fR \fIhost_name\fR
Displays only jobs that were dispatched to the specified host.
.TP
\fB-J\fR \fIjob_name\fR
Displays only jobs with the specified job name.
.TP
\fB-u\fR \fIuser_name\fR | \fB-u all\fR
Displays only jobs of the specified user, or of all users if the keyword
all is specified.
.TP
\fIjob_ID\fR | \fB"\fR\fIjob_ID\fR\fB[\fR\fIindex\fR\fB]"\fR ...
Displays only the specified jobs; more than one can be given. For a job
array, \fIjob_ID\fR displays all its elements, and
"\fIjob_ID\fR[\fIindex\fR]" displays only the specified elements. Quote
the index so that the shell does not interpret it.
.TP
\fB-t\fR
Displays the events of all jobs in time order, instead of grouping them by
job. By default, displays only the events of the last week; use -T or the
environment variable LSB_BHIST_HOURS to change the period. Can be used only
with -f and -T, and not with job IDs.
.TP
\fB-T\fR \fItime0\fR\fB,\fR\fItime1\fR
Used with -t. Displays only the events during the specified time interval.
The interval format is the same as for -C.
.TP
\fB-h\fR
Prints command usage to stderr and exits.
.TP
\fB-V\fR
Prints the Volclava version and exits.
.SH OUTPUT
.SS Default Display
One line per job, with the seconds the job spent in each state:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Summary of time in seconds spent in various states:
JOBID   USER    JOB_NAME  PEND    PSUSP   RUN     USUSP   SSUSP   UNKWN   TOTAL
1       volclav hostname  10      0       0       0       0       0       10
.fi
.RE
.TP
PEND
Time waiting before dispatch, excluding time suspended by the user.
.TP
PSUSP
Time suspended by the user while pending.
.TP
RUN
Run time.
.TP
USUSP
Time suspended by the user after dispatch.
.TP
SSUSP
Time suspended by the system after dispatch.
.TP
UNKWN
Time in unknown state, for example while sbatchd on the execution host
cannot be reached.
.TP
TOTAL
The sum of the above. For a finished job, the time from submission to
completion.
.SS -l output
The job ID, user, project and command, then the events of the job in time
order, such as submission, dispatch, start and completion, then the time
summary:
.PP
.RS
.nf
% bhist -l 631

Job <631>, User <volclava>, Project <default>, Command <sleep 123>
Thu Oct 29 10:06:59: Submitted from host <hostA>, to Queue <normal>, CWD </home/volclava/work>;
Thu Oct 29 10:07:09: Dispatched to <hostB>;
Thu Oct 29 10:07:09: Starting (Pid 9551);
Thu Oct 29 10:07:14: Running with execution home </home/volclava>, Execution CW
                     D </home/volclava/work>, Execution Pid <9551>;
Thu Oct 29 10:09:17: Done successfully. The CPU time used is 5.1 seconds;
Thu Oct 29 10:09:17: Post job process failed;

Summary of time in seconds spent in various states by  Thu Oct 29 10:09:17
  PEND     PSUSP    RUN      USUSP    SSUSP    UNKWN    TOTAL
  10       0        128      0        0        0        138
.fi
.RE
.SH BHIST SPEEDUP
If bhist speedup was enabled at build time and the administrator has started
its service, bhist sends the queries it can accelerate to the service; use
bhist as usual. A query is accelerated when it uses only -l or -w (not both),
-a, -d, -e, -p, -s, -r, -u, -q, -m, -S, -D, -C, and one or more job IDs.
Other queries, for example with -n, -f, -t or -J, run standard bhist.
.PP
An accelerated query differs from standard bhist as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
It searches all history kept in the database (the last 30 days by default).
.IP \(bu 2
New events show up a few seconds to a few tens of seconds later.
.IP \(bu 2
If the service is unavailable, bhist runs standard bhist instead and prints
"bhist speedup: falling back to standard bhist"; the results are still
correct.
.PP
See bhist-speedup(1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
LSB_BHIST_HOURS
The period, in hours, that -t displays by default. The default is 168 (one
week).
.SH FILES
.TP
LSB_SHAREDIR/logdir/lsb.events
The current event log file.
.TP
LSB_SHAREDIR/logdir/lsb.events.\fIN\fR
Archived event log files; a larger \fIN\fR is older.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. If no job matches, bhist prints "No matching job found" ("No
matching job/event found" with -t) and exits with 255.
.SH EXAMPLES
% \fBbhist\fR
.PP
Displays the time your unfinished jobs spent in each state.
.PP
% \fBbhist -l 1234\fR
.PP
Displays the detailed history of job 1234.
.PP
% \fBbhist -n 0 -l 1234\fR
.PP
Searches all event log files for job 1234 when it is no longer in the
current lsb.events.
.PP
% \fBbhist -a -u all -q normal\fR
.PP
Displays the jobs of all users submitted to the queue normal, including
finished jobs.
.PP
% \fBbhist -n 3 -C 2026/10/01/00:00,2026/10/20/23:59 -u alice\fR
.PP
Searches the three most recent event log files for jobs of alice that
finished between October 1 and October 20.
.PP
% \fBbhist -l "1234[1-10]"\fR
.PP
Displays elements 1 to 10 of job array 1234.
.PP
% \fBbhist -t -T 2026/10/27/00:00,2026/10/28/23:59\fR
.PP
Displays the events of all jobs on October 27 and 28, 2026, in time order.
.SH SEE ALSO
bjobs(1), bsub(1), bhist-speedup(1), lsb.events(5), lsb.hist(5),
lsb.params(5), badmin(8), mbatchd(8)
